00:00:06.971 --> 00:00:08.720
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: So if
I had only two words

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:12.710
to sum up how we think about
security in Google Cloud,

00:00:12.710 --> 00:00:13.580
it's this--

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:15.590
it would be trust nothing.

00:00:15.590 --> 00:00:18.770
So as you start moving your
services to Google Cloud,

00:00:18.770 --> 00:00:21.845
the first thing you do is,
obviously, secure your VPC.

00:00:21.845 --> 00:00:24.560
You'd secure
connectivity to your VPC.

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:27.850
You'd apply really
granular policies with IM--

00:00:27.850 --> 00:00:30.740
Kubernetes Engine Network
Policies, and more.

00:00:30.740 --> 00:00:32.299
And one of the most
important things

00:00:32.299 --> 00:00:34.640
that you will do after
this is to secure

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:36.890
your internet-facing apps.

00:00:36.890 --> 00:00:39.110
Now, this is
actually a tough job,

00:00:39.110 --> 00:00:42.570
because you do need to
serve your users fast.

00:00:42.570 --> 00:00:44.570
But then at the same time,
you need to block out

00:00:44.570 --> 00:00:46.970
DDoS and application attacks.

00:00:46.970 --> 00:00:49.140
And this will be the
focus of our talk today.

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:51.770
So, how can you
defend your services

00:00:51.770 --> 00:00:54.139
against a protocol,
volumetric, and then

00:00:54.139 --> 00:00:55.180
application DDoS attacks?

00:00:57.980 --> 00:01:00.500
So this is what it looks like.

00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:04.190
Now, this is
actually a graph that

00:01:04.190 --> 00:01:06.650
is drawn based on the
DDoS attacks on Google's

00:01:06.650 --> 00:01:09.470
own services, so services
such as Search, and YouTube,

00:01:09.470 --> 00:01:10.730
and Gmail.

00:01:10.730 --> 00:01:12.440
And of course, our
security experts

00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:15.410
work around the clock to
protect Google's own services

00:01:15.410 --> 00:01:16.980
from these attacks.

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:19.540
And as you can see, the attacks
are growing exponentially

00:01:19.540 --> 00:01:22.730
in volume, and not
just in this respect,

00:01:22.730 --> 00:01:24.710
but also in respect
to packets per second

00:01:24.710 --> 00:01:26.690
or any of the other parameters.

00:01:26.690 --> 00:01:30.980
And you can notice that the
scale is already past 1.7 TBps.

00:01:30.980 --> 00:01:34.940
So one of the things I did was
to ask our DDoS defense lead,

00:01:34.940 --> 00:01:39.470
like, what does absorbing a
huge DDoS attack feel like?

00:01:39.470 --> 00:01:42.530
And he says, absorbing
the large DDoS attack,

00:01:42.530 --> 00:01:44.720
it requires the
bandwidth needed to watch

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:47.990
half a million YouTube
videos all at the same time

00:01:47.990 --> 00:01:49.020
in high definition.

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:51.790
So that's what it feels like.

00:01:51.790 --> 00:01:55.600
Now, if you look at this, these
are all of the Google services

00:01:55.600 --> 00:01:58.780
and partner solutions that
you have at your disposal

00:01:58.780 --> 00:02:01.760
to go defend against
all of these attacks.

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:03.760
The only one I'm not going
to cover in this talk

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:06.280
is the last one, which
is Project Shield.

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:09.160
But that is actually
our free service

00:02:09.160 --> 00:02:12.616
that uses Google's technology
to protect new sites and then

00:02:12.616 --> 00:02:14.865
freedom of expression from
the DDoS and other attacks.

00:02:17.450 --> 00:02:20.150
So the first best
practice that we recommend

00:02:20.150 --> 00:02:23.270
for your
internet-facing services

00:02:23.270 --> 00:02:26.200
is to start with a global
load balancer and with TLS

00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:26.870
everywhere.

00:02:26.870 --> 00:02:29.420
So wherever possible, use TLS.

00:02:29.420 --> 00:02:32.030
And then as it relates
to the load balancer,

00:02:32.030 --> 00:02:35.300
you will use either our
HTTPS load balancer.

00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:38.280
Or if your traffic is non-HTTP--
so let's say you're running

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:39.410
SMDB--

00:02:39.410 --> 00:02:42.650
then you would actually run
the SSL proxy load balancer.

00:02:42.650 --> 00:02:45.650
And both of these are, again,
global load balancing flavors,

00:02:45.650 --> 00:02:48.710
which means you can put your
backends in any region you

00:02:48.710 --> 00:02:49.640
want.

00:02:49.640 --> 00:02:52.070
And you can front them
with a single VIP,

00:02:52.070 --> 00:02:53.750
which gives you
also a single point

00:02:53.750 --> 00:02:56.970
to apply all of your policies.

00:02:56.970 --> 00:03:00.410
We also have several features
including SSL policies.

00:03:00.410 --> 00:03:03.510
So you can restrict, for
instance, specific ciphers,

00:03:03.510 --> 00:03:06.560
or you can restrict
certain TLS versions.

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:12.000
And this is especially
important for PCI compliance.

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:15.870
So we put in our global
HTTP/HTTPS load balancer.

00:03:15.870 --> 00:03:17.614
We put in our policies.

00:03:17.614 --> 00:03:19.530
The next thing that you
would want to layer in

00:03:19.530 --> 00:03:20.730
is Cloud Armor.

00:03:20.730 --> 00:03:24.210
Now, this is a new service
that we introduced recently.

00:03:24.210 --> 00:03:26.647
And if you think of
what it provides,

00:03:26.647 --> 00:03:28.730
you obviously need the
[? table ?] [? stakes ?] L3

00:03:28.730 --> 00:03:31.470
and L4 DDoS defense, which is
actually really hard to defend

00:03:31.470 --> 00:03:32.610
against.

00:03:32.610 --> 00:03:35.010
In addition to that,
you need something

00:03:35.010 --> 00:03:37.560
that's very extensible,
a way you can

00:03:37.560 --> 00:03:39.540
express what your intent is.

00:03:39.540 --> 00:03:42.567
So that is why there's a policy
framework and a rules language.

00:03:42.567 --> 00:03:45.150
And then you have your features,
such as blacklist, whitelist.

00:03:45.150 --> 00:03:48.090
Or maybe you want to
control access based on geo.

00:03:48.090 --> 00:03:49.920
And then you have
your WAF protection.

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:52.232
And with all of
this, you obviously

00:03:52.232 --> 00:03:53.440
want to know what's going on.

00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:56.790
So there's the
telemetry aspect of it.

00:03:56.790 --> 00:03:59.520
And with all of this, then you
can also layer in a product

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:01.830
that we call
identity-aware proxy.

00:04:01.830 --> 00:04:05.280
So this is based on BeyondCorp,
which is how identities

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:06.990
handle inside Google.

00:04:06.990 --> 00:04:10.140
And then if you layer all
of these three together,

00:04:10.140 --> 00:04:13.560
one of the other things that
you will do is you have your VPC

00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:16.380
or you have your
instances in your VPC.

00:04:16.380 --> 00:04:19.880
And you would configure the
firewalls on those instances

00:04:19.880 --> 00:04:22.360
to only allow traffic
through the load balancer,

00:04:22.360 --> 00:04:26.110
because you put all of these
knobs at the load balancer.

00:04:26.110 --> 00:04:28.860
And you want to ensure that no
other internet-facing traffic

00:04:28.860 --> 00:04:30.215
actually leaks to your backends.

00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:36.332
So let's talk a little
bit about Cloud Armor.

00:04:36.332 --> 00:04:38.540
The first thing, as I
mentioned, is that it gives you

00:04:38.540 --> 00:04:40.260
a policy framework.

00:04:40.260 --> 00:04:45.240
What you can see here is a snip
of our GCloud console, Google

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:45.740
Console.

00:04:45.740 --> 00:04:49.430
And then you can see the three
policies that exist in there.

00:04:49.430 --> 00:04:52.410
If you start zooming
in on these policies,

00:04:52.410 --> 00:04:55.250
the first thing is you can
apply a Cloud Armor policy

00:04:55.250 --> 00:04:57.360
for a given backend service.

00:04:57.360 --> 00:04:59.480
So let's say we've
got four services.

00:04:59.480 --> 00:05:02.570
Maybe these are your four
internet-facing services.

00:05:02.570 --> 00:05:04.790
You could choose to
use this policy--

00:05:04.790 --> 00:05:07.010
let's say you've defined a
policy called Cloud Armor

00:05:07.010 --> 00:05:08.540
Policy P1.

00:05:08.540 --> 00:05:11.180
You could choose to use
it for Web-service 1.

00:05:11.180 --> 00:05:13.970
You defined a policy, P2, and
you used it for your service

00:05:13.970 --> 00:05:14.810
too.

00:05:14.810 --> 00:05:17.090
And then you defined
a policy, P3,

00:05:17.090 --> 00:05:19.850
but maybe it's applicable
to multiple services, so you

00:05:19.850 --> 00:05:22.880
can reuse the same policy
for multiple services,

00:05:22.880 --> 00:05:25.400
as you can see in there.

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:28.850
If you look closely at any
of those policies, what

00:05:28.850 --> 00:05:31.550
you would notice is that
a policy can comprise

00:05:31.550 --> 00:05:33.320
for a hierarchy of rules.

00:05:33.320 --> 00:05:35.690
And then within
each rules, you have

00:05:35.690 --> 00:05:38.012
something that you match
the traffic against.

00:05:38.012 --> 00:05:39.470
And then there's
an action that you

00:05:39.470 --> 00:05:41.390
take based on whether
there's a match or not.

00:05:41.390 --> 00:05:45.920
So the actions could be allow
the traffic, deny the traffic.

00:05:45.920 --> 00:05:48.890
We also have an interesting
option called Preview.

00:05:48.890 --> 00:05:51.860
And what that does is
lets you test out rules.

00:05:51.860 --> 00:05:55.220
So if you configure the
preview option there,

00:05:55.220 --> 00:05:57.920
it would simply log the action
but actually not take it

00:05:57.920 --> 00:05:59.190
on your traffic.

00:05:59.190 --> 00:06:00.770
So if you're testing
all the rules,

00:06:00.770 --> 00:06:05.470
that's probably what you would
start with to begin with.

00:06:05.470 --> 00:06:08.370
Now, when we started talking
about building [INAUDIBLE]

00:06:08.370 --> 00:06:11.450
almost a way to go
express the rules,

00:06:11.450 --> 00:06:14.250
we thought about using a
variety of different tools.

00:06:14.250 --> 00:06:15.870
Some are proprietary.

00:06:15.870 --> 00:06:18.660
But then we decided to
use something that's not

00:06:18.660 --> 00:06:21.030
just flexible, but also open.

00:06:21.030 --> 00:06:24.630
And we settled on CEL, which
is short for Common Expression

00:06:24.630 --> 00:06:25.920
Language.

00:06:25.920 --> 00:06:28.680
If any of you have
used Firebase,

00:06:28.680 --> 00:06:29.940
you've already used CEL.

00:06:29.940 --> 00:06:33.510
So that is what it's used for,
expressing certain expressions.

00:06:33.510 --> 00:06:36.970
And then once you specify the
rules using this language,

00:06:36.970 --> 00:06:39.090
Cloud Armor will
then use these rules

00:06:39.090 --> 00:06:42.907
to go orchestrate the underlying
global infrastructure.

00:06:42.907 --> 00:06:44.490
And then obviously
that infrastructure

00:06:44.490 --> 00:06:46.315
is going to allow or
block your service,

00:06:46.315 --> 00:06:48.690
based on whatever it is you
have configured for the rule.

00:06:51.292 --> 00:06:53.500
So what are the types of
things that you can actually

00:06:53.500 --> 00:06:55.420
do with Cloud Armor?

00:06:55.420 --> 00:06:57.160
The first thing
that you can do is

00:06:57.160 --> 00:07:01.450
you can either allow or block
traffic for IPs or CIDRs.

00:07:01.450 --> 00:07:05.431
And this could be either IPv4
addresses or CIDRs or IPv6

00:07:05.431 --> 00:07:05.930
as well.

00:07:05.930 --> 00:07:08.140
So we support both.

00:07:08.140 --> 00:07:10.240
If you think of the
other types of things

00:07:10.240 --> 00:07:12.730
that you might want to
do, a very common ask

00:07:12.730 --> 00:07:15.160
is for customers to
be able to control

00:07:15.160 --> 00:07:17.050
based on the geolocation.

00:07:17.050 --> 00:07:18.700
So that is what you see in here.

00:07:18.700 --> 00:07:21.310
And you can combine it with
IP addresses using operators

00:07:21.310 --> 00:07:24.130
like and or not.

00:07:24.130 --> 00:07:25.930
One of the other
popular things is,

00:07:25.930 --> 00:07:28.090
how do we defend
ourselves against all

00:07:28.090 --> 00:07:29.885
of these application attacks?

00:07:29.885 --> 00:07:32.260
And so that is what you see
what the cross-site scripting

00:07:32.260 --> 00:07:35.340
and the SQL injection defense.

00:07:35.340 --> 00:07:38.290
This is where, again, you
can see the rules language

00:07:38.290 --> 00:07:39.276
in action.

00:07:39.276 --> 00:07:40.900
So there's a custom
rule that's crafted

00:07:40.900 --> 00:07:42.880
where it's looking
for US, and then it's

00:07:42.880 --> 00:07:45.590
looking for certain
things in the user-agent.

00:07:45.590 --> 00:07:47.770
And then it's looking for
a certain type of cookie.

00:07:47.770 --> 00:07:50.920
And then you could choose to
either deny or permit along

00:07:50.920 --> 00:07:53.025
with this one.

00:07:53.025 --> 00:07:54.400
And then the last
important thing

00:07:54.400 --> 00:07:56.950
is once your traffic
is allowed or blocked,

00:07:56.950 --> 00:07:59.167
it would go lock
this in Stackdriver.

00:07:59.167 --> 00:08:01.750
And then it will tell you which
is the policy that it matched,

00:08:01.750 --> 00:08:02.500
which is the rule.

00:08:02.500 --> 00:08:05.380
So the priority is actually
the rule number and then what

00:08:05.380 --> 00:08:07.300
was the action that was taken.

00:08:07.300 --> 00:08:09.910
So once you have all
of this in place,

00:08:09.910 --> 00:08:12.430
it is very easy to
allow or block traffic

00:08:12.430 --> 00:08:16.300
based on your
unique requirements.

00:08:16.300 --> 00:08:18.679
So I wanted to actually
give you a quick look

00:08:18.679 --> 00:08:19.720
at what's under the hood.

00:08:23.080 --> 00:08:25.350
The first one is actually
pretty straightforward,

00:08:25.350 --> 00:08:28.060
where essentially
for the L3, L4,

00:08:28.060 --> 00:08:30.130
you will turn on the
global DDoS defense.

00:08:30.130 --> 00:08:32.140
In here, let's turn
on this defense,

00:08:32.140 --> 00:08:36.440
because what I want to show you
is actually what's coming next.

00:08:36.440 --> 00:08:39.789
The second thing is
let's go and launch

00:08:39.789 --> 00:08:42.640
an SQL injection, or "sequel"
injection, as some of you

00:08:42.640 --> 00:08:43.610
call it.

00:08:43.610 --> 00:08:46.960
And in here, it's actually worse
than just bringing your service

00:08:46.960 --> 00:08:47.770
down.

00:08:47.770 --> 00:08:52.490
Because it will actually go and
also try to steal your data.

00:08:52.490 --> 00:08:55.210
So I wanted to just walk you
through the backend of this

00:08:55.210 --> 00:08:59.170
to show you what it looks like,
which is, let's go in here.

00:08:59.170 --> 00:09:02.920
This is the actual UI here.

00:09:02.920 --> 00:09:07.786
You would go to the [? stack ?]
called Network Security.

00:09:07.786 --> 00:09:09.160
And then you can
see that we have

00:09:09.160 --> 00:09:10.497
a bunch of different policies.

00:09:10.497 --> 00:09:12.580
So these could have been
just rules in our policy.

00:09:12.580 --> 00:09:15.310
Or they can be different
policies itself.

00:09:15.310 --> 00:09:17.360
Let's look at the one
for SQL injection.

00:09:17.360 --> 00:09:20.320
Now, if you see in here,
the most important one

00:09:20.320 --> 00:09:22.870
is the one you see in
here, which is evaluate

00:09:22.870 --> 00:09:24.550
preconfigured expression.

00:09:24.550 --> 00:09:27.100
So you just need one rule,
and then under the hood,

00:09:27.100 --> 00:09:30.280
it's going to look for 160
patterns to actually go

00:09:30.280 --> 00:09:34.220
and then block the traffic
of a SQL injection.

00:09:34.220 --> 00:09:36.010
The other policy that
I wanted to show you

00:09:36.010 --> 00:09:37.570
is the one that I
showed you briefly

00:09:37.570 --> 00:09:40.330
on screen, which is this one.

00:09:40.330 --> 00:09:43.110
And then if you see in
here, the first rule-- so

00:09:43.110 --> 00:09:46.280
let's go and try and edit that.

00:09:46.280 --> 00:09:49.100
So if you look at this rule,
and I see if I can zoom in

00:09:49.100 --> 00:09:50.820
so you can see it better.

00:09:50.820 --> 00:09:54.780
So when we start looking at
our rules, we have two modes.

00:09:54.780 --> 00:09:57.590
One of them is essentially
a basic mode, which

00:09:57.590 --> 00:09:59.400
is the one you see on top.

00:09:59.400 --> 00:10:02.990
And that's simply if you want to
add a blacklist or a whitelist.

00:10:02.990 --> 00:10:06.150
What is most important is
actually this advanced mode,

00:10:06.150 --> 00:10:07.820
which is what you see here.

00:10:07.820 --> 00:10:10.850
And then the entire rules
language that you see actually

00:10:10.850 --> 00:10:12.570
can be used here.

00:10:12.570 --> 00:10:14.630
So this was the
specific example where

00:10:14.630 --> 00:10:17.450
you would look for
traffic, which is from US,

00:10:17.450 --> 00:10:20.430
and then whether user and
agent contains bad bot.

00:10:20.430 --> 00:10:22.940
And then the request
headers, the cookie itself,

00:10:22.940 --> 00:10:26.720
contains the specific value
that you see in there.

00:10:26.720 --> 00:10:28.910
Some of the other things
I wanted to point out

00:10:28.910 --> 00:10:31.820
is, as I mentioned,
there was an allow.

00:10:31.820 --> 00:10:36.080
If you do a deny, you can also
look through several of these

00:10:36.080 --> 00:10:38.840
and then figure out what is
the status quo that you want.

00:10:38.840 --> 00:10:42.350
And then the Preview mode
you can simply enable here.

00:10:42.350 --> 00:10:45.590
And then the priority would
tell its order or its place

00:10:45.590 --> 00:10:46.770
inside the hierarchy.

00:10:46.770 --> 00:10:50.220
So you can see that
on the right here.

00:10:50.220 --> 00:10:54.890
So that was basically a look
at what the policies look like.

00:10:54.890 --> 00:10:58.070
And then switching back to
Cloud Armor and then some

00:10:58.070 --> 00:11:00.240
of the things that
we do with it,

00:11:00.240 --> 00:11:02.900
one of the most interesting
things that you can actually

00:11:02.900 --> 00:11:05.390
do with Cloud Armor
is to combine it

00:11:05.390 --> 00:11:07.970
with several of
Google's technologies

00:11:07.970 --> 00:11:11.300
and also combine it with several
of our partner solutions.

00:11:11.300 --> 00:11:13.160
So while we won't
have enough time

00:11:13.160 --> 00:11:15.890
to go through solutions
from each of these partners,

00:11:15.890 --> 00:11:18.950
you can see that it ranges from
identity and access management

00:11:18.950 --> 00:11:22.610
to scanning to
governance, compliance,

00:11:22.610 --> 00:11:24.620
even we have like CASBs.

00:11:24.620 --> 00:11:27.690
We've got things against
phishing and so on.

00:11:27.690 --> 00:11:31.400
So on our website, we actually
describe each of these partner

00:11:31.400 --> 00:11:34.280
solutions in great detail.

00:11:34.280 --> 00:11:36.860
One of the partners I
specifically wanted to call out

00:11:36.860 --> 00:11:38.240
is Reblaze.

00:11:38.240 --> 00:11:41.820
And Reblaze provides a
web security solution.

00:11:41.820 --> 00:11:45.440
So it's in use at eBay and
several of our other customers.

00:11:45.440 --> 00:11:47.220
And then, of course,
it provides a WAF.

00:11:47.220 --> 00:11:49.500
It will provide DDoS
protection, anti scraping.

00:11:49.500 --> 00:11:52.190
It will provide
fine-grained ACLs.

00:11:52.190 --> 00:11:55.670
One of the really interesting
bits about Reblaze is this--

00:11:55.670 --> 00:11:59.810
that the architecture is
actually fully powered by GCP,

00:11:59.810 --> 00:12:02.870
along with a lot of
value-added intelligence

00:12:02.870 --> 00:12:05.460
that Reblaze brings
to the solution.

00:12:05.460 --> 00:12:07.130
So I wanted to put
this here so you

00:12:07.130 --> 00:12:09.020
can look at some of the
foundational building

00:12:09.020 --> 00:12:11.180
blocks that come from GCP.

00:12:11.180 --> 00:12:13.200
Specifically, there
are five services.

00:12:13.200 --> 00:12:14.870
So there's global
load balancing.

00:12:14.870 --> 00:12:16.280
There is CDN.

00:12:16.280 --> 00:12:17.440
There is Cloud Armor.

00:12:17.440 --> 00:12:18.280
There is BigQuery.

00:12:18.280 --> 00:12:19.940
And there's machine learning.

00:12:19.940 --> 00:12:21.710
Now, the global load
balancing provides

00:12:21.710 --> 00:12:24.390
the Edge infrastructure to
absorb layer three and layer

00:12:24.390 --> 00:12:25.580
four DDoS attacks.

00:12:25.580 --> 00:12:29.720
And it also provides a
point to TLS termination.

00:12:29.720 --> 00:12:32.476
Now, the BigQuery is combined
with machine learning.

00:12:32.476 --> 00:12:34.100
And then the kinds
of things it will do

00:12:34.100 --> 00:12:38.190
is like traffic analysis in real
time or application analysis.

00:12:38.190 --> 00:12:41.270
So this is where you want to
[? profile ?] legitimate users,

00:12:41.270 --> 00:12:43.940
their input, and then make sure
that it is something that you

00:12:43.940 --> 00:12:45.590
can feed back into the WAF.

00:12:45.590 --> 00:12:48.480
It'll do real-time
biometric data analysis.

00:12:48.480 --> 00:12:50.090
So this is used to
tell, for instance,

00:12:50.090 --> 00:12:52.250
bots apart from humans.

00:12:52.250 --> 00:12:55.790
And any time once it
detects something,

00:12:55.790 --> 00:12:59.010
it'll use the Cloud Armor
things, such as blacklist.

00:12:59.010 --> 00:13:01.850
So your signatures, and
it'll delegate Cloud Armor

00:13:01.850 --> 00:13:03.320
wherever applicable.

00:13:03.320 --> 00:13:05.480
And then what the main
thing it'll give you

00:13:05.480 --> 00:13:08.360
is essentially defense
of the Edge with this.

00:13:08.360 --> 00:13:10.490
And then your traffic
or your back traffic

00:13:10.490 --> 00:13:12.680
will never reach your VPC.

00:13:12.680 --> 00:13:15.470
And then you've got the
Reblaze proxy instances, which

00:13:15.470 --> 00:13:17.030
will run all of
the Reblaze value

00:13:17.030 --> 00:13:19.400
added features that wouldn't
get delegated to any

00:13:19.400 --> 00:13:20.820
of these systems.

00:13:20.820 --> 00:13:22.760
So the reason I
wanted to show you

00:13:22.760 --> 00:13:25.580
this is because this
shows what is possible

00:13:25.580 --> 00:13:28.020
using several of
Google's technologies,

00:13:28.020 --> 00:13:30.860
especially Global LB,
Cloud Armor, other GCP

00:13:30.860 --> 00:13:35.026
services, to harden your own
posture for your own services.

00:13:35.026 --> 00:13:36.650
And then, of course,
you can complement

00:13:36.650 --> 00:13:39.500
each of these with
solutions from our partners.

00:13:39.500 --> 00:13:42.800
So this was a whirlwind
tour of Cloud Armor.

00:13:42.800 --> 00:13:45.180
And I know we spoke
about partners.

00:13:45.180 --> 00:13:48.840
One of the other things I wanted
to talk about is our customers.

00:13:48.840 --> 00:13:52.130
And I think most of you are
aware that we announced Target

00:13:52.130 --> 00:13:54.590
as our customer
at this GCP Next.

00:13:54.590 --> 00:13:56.390
And with that, I'm
delighted to invite

00:13:56.390 --> 00:13:59.120
Nick, our lead
engineer for Target,

00:13:59.120 --> 00:14:01.460
to come and describe how
to think about application

00:14:01.460 --> 00:14:03.270
security at Target.

00:14:03.270 --> 00:14:03.770
Nick?

00:14:03.770 --> 00:14:04.662
[APPLAUSE]

00:14:04.662 --> 00:14:05.662
NICK JACQUES: Thank you.

00:14:08.980 --> 00:14:10.434
Thanks, Prajakta.

00:14:10.434 --> 00:14:11.260
Hi.

00:14:11.260 --> 00:14:12.700
So my name's Nick Jacques.

00:14:12.700 --> 00:14:16.870
I'm a lead engineer at Target
on our Cloud Platform team.

00:14:16.870 --> 00:14:18.940
Just to give you a little
context around who I am

00:14:18.940 --> 00:14:22.920
and what Target is, so Target's
a pretty popular US retailer.

00:14:22.920 --> 00:14:25.750
We have over 1,800
stores in the US.

00:14:25.750 --> 00:14:28.410
We also have a good amount
of distribution centers

00:14:28.410 --> 00:14:30.670
and several
headquarters locations

00:14:30.670 --> 00:14:32.917
and a handful of data centers.

00:14:32.917 --> 00:14:34.750
I've been working on
our cloud platform team

00:14:34.750 --> 00:14:37.510
for about three
years now, and I want

00:14:37.510 --> 00:14:40.510
to provide a little
context around timelines.

00:14:40.510 --> 00:14:44.650
Last year, the very, very
beginning of 2017, we

00:14:44.650 --> 00:14:48.070
kind of embarked on this
journey to migrate our workloads

00:14:48.070 --> 00:14:53.050
from a variety of cloud
providers into GCP.

00:14:53.050 --> 00:14:55.450
We were able to do
that prior to what

00:14:55.450 --> 00:14:59.410
we call peak season in 2017,
so before fall comes around

00:14:59.410 --> 00:15:02.110
and Black Friday and
all kinds of fun times

00:15:02.110 --> 00:15:03.880
around the holidays.

00:15:03.880 --> 00:15:06.070
And things went really well.

00:15:06.070 --> 00:15:09.760
So at the beginning of
2018, we thought, well,

00:15:09.760 --> 00:15:12.010
we've migrated the
non-commerce parts of our site,

00:15:12.010 --> 00:15:15.220
so the parts of the site that
will help you search or browse

00:15:15.220 --> 00:15:18.650
to find a product or will
display product images

00:15:18.650 --> 00:15:20.477
or reviews for you.

00:15:20.477 --> 00:15:22.060
What about the
commerce part, the part

00:15:22.060 --> 00:15:24.850
that accepts credit card
numbers, the part that

00:15:24.850 --> 00:15:27.740
lets you check out on the site,
all kinds of things like that?

00:15:27.740 --> 00:15:30.520
And we thought, this is
something we should do too.

00:15:30.520 --> 00:15:33.310
Our commerce environment
was hosted in a dedicated

00:15:33.310 --> 00:15:34.910
[? colo ?] facility.

00:15:34.910 --> 00:15:37.060
And so we thought it was
time to modernize that.

00:15:37.060 --> 00:15:40.630
So at the beginning
of 2018, around March,

00:15:40.630 --> 00:15:42.380
we embarked on that journey.

00:15:42.380 --> 00:15:44.380
And we're actually almost
entirely done with it.

00:15:44.380 --> 00:15:48.430
So the vast part of
target.com, our mobile apps,

00:15:48.430 --> 00:15:55.520
and a good piece of our APIs
as well have backends in GCP.

00:15:55.520 --> 00:15:58.450
So moving on, let's
just kind of do

00:15:58.450 --> 00:16:01.000
a little overview of Target's
architectural patterns.

00:16:01.000 --> 00:16:03.400
So the way that we've
kind of built things out

00:16:03.400 --> 00:16:07.120
is that we basically have a
CDN that fronts everything

00:16:07.120 --> 00:16:08.390
that we have on the web.

00:16:08.390 --> 00:16:11.080
So all of our traffic
comes through the CDN.

00:16:11.080 --> 00:16:13.000
We apply a variety
of useful features

00:16:13.000 --> 00:16:15.670
that the CDN provides,
things like caching, web app

00:16:15.670 --> 00:16:19.650
firewalls, a massive
amount of logging.

00:16:19.650 --> 00:16:22.030
And it also provides us
with some defense tactics.

00:16:22.030 --> 00:16:24.657
So there we can
do rate limiting,

00:16:24.657 --> 00:16:25.740
and we can also blacklist.

00:16:25.740 --> 00:16:27.380
So if we're seeing
malicious traffic

00:16:27.380 --> 00:16:29.390
or if we're receiving
large volumes of traffic,

00:16:29.390 --> 00:16:31.030
we can handle it there.

00:16:31.030 --> 00:16:33.220
The CDN is also useful
for us because Target

00:16:33.220 --> 00:16:34.850
has a multi-cloud strategy.

00:16:34.850 --> 00:16:37.090
So we have a pretty
big footprint in GCP,

00:16:37.090 --> 00:16:40.330
but we don't want to paint
ourselves into the corner.

00:16:40.330 --> 00:16:45.380
And so we want the ability to
use other CSPs or, for example,

00:16:45.380 --> 00:16:47.680
if we have a trusted third
party provider for pieces

00:16:47.680 --> 00:16:49.900
of our site or
certain functionality,

00:16:49.900 --> 00:16:53.150
we want to be able to direct
that traffic to them as needed.

00:16:53.150 --> 00:16:56.050
And then finally, the third
piece in this architecture

00:16:56.050 --> 00:16:59.290
is using the L7 load balancer.

00:16:59.290 --> 00:17:03.310
So I had a previous talk
just a few hours ago,

00:17:03.310 --> 00:17:06.040
where we went into a really
deep dive about Target's

00:17:06.040 --> 00:17:07.599
architecture in GCP.

00:17:07.599 --> 00:17:10.359
Unfortunately, no one offers
time travel as a service

00:17:10.359 --> 00:17:12.386
yet, so we can't go back.

00:17:12.386 --> 00:17:13.760
But it will be
posted on YouTube.

00:17:13.760 --> 00:17:16.390
So if you want a really deep
dive into Target's architecture

00:17:16.390 --> 00:17:19.420
and how we're using a lot of the
network functionality of GCP,

00:17:19.420 --> 00:17:21.220
I would encourage you
to check that out.

00:17:21.220 --> 00:17:23.380
And in there,
you'll hear me just

00:17:23.380 --> 00:17:24.970
gush about all
the amazing things

00:17:24.970 --> 00:17:26.380
that these low balancers do.

00:17:26.380 --> 00:17:28.569
We don't have to worry
about prewarming or scaling.

00:17:28.569 --> 00:17:31.600
We don't have to worry about
regionalized load balancers,

00:17:31.600 --> 00:17:33.730
and then doing
geolocation at the Edge

00:17:33.730 --> 00:17:36.540
and trying to figure out
which load balancer is closest

00:17:36.540 --> 00:17:37.790
to the user, things like that.

00:17:37.790 --> 00:17:39.910
So it works really great for us.

00:17:39.910 --> 00:17:42.580
One of the amazing things about
this that our teams really

00:17:42.580 --> 00:17:44.352
enjoy is the ability
to scale based

00:17:44.352 --> 00:17:46.810
on requests per second coming
in through the load balancer.

00:17:46.810 --> 00:17:49.720
So this is especially great
if you have services that,

00:17:49.720 --> 00:17:52.210
for example, don't hit the CPU.

00:17:52.210 --> 00:17:54.430
So you can't really
auto scale based on CPU.

00:17:54.430 --> 00:17:56.860
But you know that a
given instance can only

00:17:56.860 --> 00:17:58.900
handle so much traffic,
so you can scale out

00:17:58.900 --> 00:18:00.670
horizontally the
more requests are

00:18:00.670 --> 00:18:02.530
coming into your load balancer.

00:18:02.530 --> 00:18:04.739
So let's move past
that just a little bit.

00:18:04.739 --> 00:18:07.030
And now we're going to start
talking about Cloud Armor.

00:18:07.030 --> 00:18:08.680
So a really important
thing for Target

00:18:08.680 --> 00:18:10.430
is that security
is for everyone.

00:18:10.430 --> 00:18:11.710
It's for our team members.

00:18:11.710 --> 00:18:12.970
It's for our guests.

00:18:12.970 --> 00:18:15.520
It's for our team members
who are our guests.

00:18:15.520 --> 00:18:17.440
It's for everyone
that we interact with.

00:18:17.440 --> 00:18:19.315
So no matter what you're
doing on target.com,

00:18:19.315 --> 00:18:21.010
or on the mobile
apps, or even using

00:18:21.010 --> 00:18:25.130
our APIs at api.target.com,
security is really important.

00:18:25.130 --> 00:18:27.560
And this is where Cloud Armor
starts to come into play.

00:18:27.560 --> 00:18:30.430
So something was missing
for us last year.

00:18:30.430 --> 00:18:32.650
Cloud Armor wasn't
generally available yet,

00:18:32.650 --> 00:18:34.584
and so we were kind of
sitting there thinking,

00:18:34.584 --> 00:18:37.000
hey, these load balancers,
these all seven load balancers,

00:18:37.000 --> 00:18:38.750
are really great, but
something's missing.

00:18:38.750 --> 00:18:41.080
We can't apply
firewall rules to them.

00:18:41.080 --> 00:18:43.990
So that means that we don't
have a way to only allow traffic

00:18:43.990 --> 00:18:45.970
from our CDN, for instance.

00:18:45.970 --> 00:18:49.100
We didn't really have a good
method of ingress control.

00:18:49.100 --> 00:18:51.950
And like anything you
put out on the internet,

00:18:51.950 --> 00:18:54.384
we began to see some kind
of suspicious traffic.

00:18:54.384 --> 00:18:56.050
You know, obviously
some of it's benign.

00:18:56.050 --> 00:18:57.790
It's people just kind of
port knocking and seeing

00:18:57.790 --> 00:18:59.170
what's going on out there.

00:18:59.170 --> 00:19:01.280
Some of it, not so much.

00:19:01.280 --> 00:19:02.920
So here you can see
just a quick snip

00:19:02.920 --> 00:19:06.890
from Stackdriver logs of
traffic that is denied--

00:19:06.890 --> 00:19:10.850
a lot of attempts to try
to get after phpMyADmin.

00:19:10.850 --> 00:19:13.360
I took a little time, and I
tried to catalog some of these.

00:19:13.360 --> 00:19:15.860
And now, this isn't a definitive
list or anything like this,

00:19:15.860 --> 00:19:21.490
but obviously we saw quite
a bit of different attempts

00:19:21.490 --> 00:19:25.600
to see if we had
phpMyAdmin, or WebDAV,

00:19:25.600 --> 00:19:27.770
or even Drupal installed.

00:19:27.770 --> 00:19:30.640
And so luckily, none
of this is actually

00:19:30.640 --> 00:19:32.210
making it to our instances.

00:19:32.210 --> 00:19:35.850
And our instances don't run
these pieces of applications.

00:19:35.850 --> 00:19:37.480
But this still
isn't stuff that we

00:19:37.480 --> 00:19:39.660
want coming into our
instances in the first place.

00:19:39.660 --> 00:19:41.410
We don't want people
knocking on our door,

00:19:41.410 --> 00:19:43.000
seeing what's going on there.

00:19:43.000 --> 00:19:46.900
So this kind of moves into
knowing and understanding

00:19:46.900 --> 00:19:48.520
traffic patterns.

00:19:48.520 --> 00:19:50.380
Obviously there's a
pretty wide spectrum.

00:19:50.380 --> 00:19:51.970
In some cases, in
some industries,

00:19:51.970 --> 00:19:53.890
you can classify things
as known good based

00:19:53.890 --> 00:19:57.825
on user interactions, or source
addresses, or things like that.

00:19:57.825 --> 00:19:59.950
And in some cases, you can
classify things as known

00:19:59.950 --> 00:20:02.350
bad, again, based on
sources or maybe you

00:20:02.350 --> 00:20:04.234
set a cookie or
something like that.

00:20:04.234 --> 00:20:05.650
And for a lot of
industries, there

00:20:05.650 --> 00:20:08.920
is a pretty wide, gray
unknown in the middle.

00:20:08.920 --> 00:20:12.490
In Target's case, luckily,
we actually kind of fall over

00:20:12.490 --> 00:20:13.750
on the left hand side.

00:20:13.750 --> 00:20:16.540
And the reason for that is
we have a pretty limited list

00:20:16.540 --> 00:20:19.420
of criteria of what
is good traffic to us.

00:20:19.420 --> 00:20:21.700
That is traffic coming
from our CDN provider.

00:20:21.700 --> 00:20:25.520
That is traffic from our
headquarters and data centers.

00:20:25.520 --> 00:20:28.300
It's traffic from
other GCP projects that

00:20:28.300 --> 00:20:32.360
is egressing and hitting
api.target.com, for instance,

00:20:32.360 --> 00:20:33.820
and some trusted third parties.

00:20:33.820 --> 00:20:37.142
And then literally everything
else, we don't want.

00:20:37.142 --> 00:20:39.100
So we don't want the
internet to come knocking.

00:20:39.100 --> 00:20:43.060
We don't want adventurous
and crafty people trying

00:20:43.060 --> 00:20:45.190
to figure out what
sales we're doing ahead

00:20:45.190 --> 00:20:47.090
of time, things like that.

00:20:47.090 --> 00:20:50.300
So this is where Cloud
Armor comes into play.

00:20:50.300 --> 00:20:53.112
So just to give you kind
of a graphic view of this,

00:20:53.112 --> 00:20:54.820
this is kind of our
overall architecture.

00:20:54.820 --> 00:20:57.700
So we have the ability for
Target guests, and even people

00:20:57.700 --> 00:21:00.490
who aren't guests or
applications and computers who

00:21:00.490 --> 00:21:04.001
aren't guests, to access our
services through the CDN Edge.

00:21:04.001 --> 00:21:06.250
And that will eventually
make its way through and then

00:21:06.250 --> 00:21:07.930
come into our load balancers.

00:21:07.930 --> 00:21:10.270
Now, what we don't
want to have happen

00:21:10.270 --> 00:21:12.700
is this other traffic kind
of coming in from the side

00:21:12.700 --> 00:21:16.420
and going through or bypassing
the architecture that we've

00:21:16.420 --> 00:21:17.950
so carefully set up.

00:21:17.950 --> 00:21:21.550
So what's really useful in
this particular instance

00:21:21.550 --> 00:21:25.420
is basically applying Cloud
Armor just at the load balancer

00:21:25.420 --> 00:21:26.060
there.

00:21:26.060 --> 00:21:28.630
And so when this traffic
tries to come through again,

00:21:28.630 --> 00:21:31.630
Cloud Armor just goes ahead
and blocks that for us.

00:21:31.630 --> 00:21:34.090
So eventually, all the good
traffic will come through.

00:21:34.090 --> 00:21:37.420
It will hit the instances or
our Kubernetes pods as intended.

00:21:37.420 --> 00:21:41.230
And those backends will never
see that traffic that we just

00:21:41.230 --> 00:21:43.400
don't want coming through.

00:21:43.400 --> 00:21:46.186
So once we start talking
about using Cloud Armor,

00:21:46.186 --> 00:21:47.560
something that's
really important

00:21:47.560 --> 00:21:50.800
is how you actually are
going to use your policies.

00:21:50.800 --> 00:21:53.050
We actually have two
different environments,

00:21:53.050 --> 00:21:54.050
as I mentioned earlier--

00:21:54.050 --> 00:21:56.440
our non-commerce environment
and our commerce environment.

00:21:56.440 --> 00:21:58.356
And they're laid out a
little bit differently.

00:21:58.356 --> 00:22:00.170
So our non-commerce
environments,

00:22:00.170 --> 00:22:04.150
they're basically large,
monolithic projects

00:22:04.150 --> 00:22:05.530
that are multitenant.

00:22:05.530 --> 00:22:07.690
So we have many
different applications

00:22:07.690 --> 00:22:09.490
running in these projects.

00:22:09.490 --> 00:22:11.410
The advantage in
a single project

00:22:11.410 --> 00:22:15.190
is you can deploy one
Cloud Armor security policy

00:22:15.190 --> 00:22:17.980
and have that apply to all
of your Edge load balancers.

00:22:17.980 --> 00:22:20.877
So for your deployments that
have many load balancers,

00:22:20.877 --> 00:22:22.960
this is really advantageous,
because you basically

00:22:22.960 --> 00:22:26.560
have centralized management
of those security policies.

00:22:26.560 --> 00:22:28.240
Or if you have
special requirements

00:22:28.240 --> 00:22:29.980
for a certain few
load balancers,

00:22:29.980 --> 00:22:33.880
you can create one-off policies
and apply those as well.

00:22:33.880 --> 00:22:35.650
If you have multiple
projects-- and this

00:22:35.650 --> 00:22:37.316
is our case in our
commerce environment,

00:22:37.316 --> 00:22:40.510
because we're using a
feature called shared VPC.

00:22:40.510 --> 00:22:43.720
So in this type of
configuration, what happens is

00:22:43.720 --> 00:22:45.940
we have multiple
projects that contain

00:22:45.940 --> 00:22:47.710
just the applications
that are intended

00:22:47.710 --> 00:22:49.180
for that particular project.

00:22:49.180 --> 00:22:51.580
And those are bound to their
own individual subnets.

00:22:51.580 --> 00:22:54.190
Each project and each
application in those projects

00:22:54.190 --> 00:22:56.140
has their own load balancers.

00:22:56.140 --> 00:22:58.090
And so we need to
distribute those policies

00:22:58.090 --> 00:22:59.920
to each one of those
service projects

00:22:59.920 --> 00:23:01.840
and then bind them to
the load balancers.

00:23:01.840 --> 00:23:04.490
So that means things are
a little more distributed.

00:23:04.490 --> 00:23:07.780
And it could be a little more
difficult for you to manage.

00:23:07.780 --> 00:23:09.470
Luckily for us, things
work pretty well.

00:23:09.470 --> 00:23:12.490
We have a centralized
Terraform repository

00:23:12.490 --> 00:23:14.710
that contains all of
our Cloud Armor rules.

00:23:14.710 --> 00:23:16.090
And so we're able
to deploy those

00:23:16.090 --> 00:23:20.720
across our suite of service
projects pretty quickly.

00:23:20.720 --> 00:23:22.300
So just to go
through a blueprint

00:23:22.300 --> 00:23:23.980
of how we're doing things.

00:23:23.980 --> 00:23:25.990
In our non-commerce
environment, we essentially

00:23:25.990 --> 00:23:29.770
have a single policy for
all of our load balancers.

00:23:29.770 --> 00:23:31.900
That policy is effectively
what I had just

00:23:31.900 --> 00:23:33.980
mentioned a few slides ago.

00:23:33.980 --> 00:23:37.360
We use terraform to
centrally manage that policy.

00:23:37.360 --> 00:23:41.290
And our application teams and
our platform services teams

00:23:41.290 --> 00:23:44.440
use an open source tool called
Spinnaker to create and manage

00:23:44.440 --> 00:23:47.920
their load balancers and
also to deploy instances.

00:23:47.920 --> 00:23:50.650
We currently have an
out-of-band process

00:23:50.650 --> 00:23:54.080
that goes through and attaches
security policies to those load

00:23:54.080 --> 00:23:57.010
balancers created
through Spinnaker.

00:23:57.010 --> 00:24:00.574
In our commerce environment,
things are a little different.

00:24:00.574 --> 00:24:02.740
We're much more aggressive
about the security policy

00:24:02.740 --> 00:24:04.810
there-- or posture, obviously.

00:24:04.810 --> 00:24:08.465
So what we do is we have a
policy per service project.

00:24:08.465 --> 00:24:10.460
And that will attach
to the load balancers

00:24:10.460 --> 00:24:12.390
in that given the
service project.

00:24:12.390 --> 00:24:15.300
Again, we use Terraform
to manage these policies.

00:24:15.300 --> 00:24:17.700
And what we do is we actually
abstract these policies

00:24:17.700 --> 00:24:18.780
from our tenants.

00:24:18.780 --> 00:24:22.140
So when our tenants use
Terraform to create their load

00:24:22.140 --> 00:24:24.000
balancers, what's
actually happening

00:24:24.000 --> 00:24:27.087
is there's a Terraform module
under the hood that contains

00:24:27.087 --> 00:24:28.920
the Cloud Armor policies,
and it attaches it

00:24:28.920 --> 00:24:30.150
to the load balancer.

00:24:30.150 --> 00:24:33.180
So effectively, when we have
one of our application teams

00:24:33.180 --> 00:24:35.250
create a load balancer
through Terraform,

00:24:35.250 --> 00:24:38.010
the Cloud Armor security
policy and SSL policies

00:24:38.010 --> 00:24:40.410
are automatically
applied like day one.

00:24:40.410 --> 00:24:43.080
So there is never a concern
about a load balancer

00:24:43.080 --> 00:24:46.790
that wouldn't have
these policies in place.

00:24:46.790 --> 00:24:49.330
So then just to kind of
wrap things up there,

00:24:49.330 --> 00:24:52.540
with our load balancers, we take
all of the Stackdriver logs,

00:24:52.540 --> 00:24:55.690
including all of the Cloud
Armor logs, and what we do

00:24:55.690 --> 00:24:59.080
is we use logging syncs to bring
those back to our data center.

00:24:59.080 --> 00:25:02.560
Once in our data center, they
go through a series of Kafka

00:25:02.560 --> 00:25:04.210
topics and subscriptions.

00:25:04.210 --> 00:25:07.250
And we ingest them into Target's
security infrastructure.

00:25:07.250 --> 00:25:10.240
So using that and looking at
the type of traffic that's

00:25:10.240 --> 00:25:12.910
being blocked by Cloud
Armor, we can actually

00:25:12.910 --> 00:25:15.370
gain some insights into
the type of traffic that's

00:25:15.370 --> 00:25:17.410
happening there and detect
any possible attacks

00:25:17.410 --> 00:25:19.960
that someone may be launching.

00:25:19.960 --> 00:25:26.470
So if we rewind to our cloud
journey in GCP, in fall 2017,

00:25:26.470 --> 00:25:29.360
Cloud Armor was in its
pretty early stages.

00:25:29.360 --> 00:25:32.590
But we wanted to have
a defense mechanism

00:25:32.590 --> 00:25:34.060
should anything occur.

00:25:34.060 --> 00:25:37.450
So again, we were
ingesting logs.

00:25:37.450 --> 00:25:41.140
And our security operations team
was basically analyzing those

00:25:41.140 --> 00:25:42.910
and would tell us
real time if we needed

00:25:42.910 --> 00:25:45.310
to take any sort of action.

00:25:45.310 --> 00:25:49.030
Our Cloud Armor policy at the
time was tactical blacklisting.

00:25:49.030 --> 00:25:51.280
So by that what I mean
is we had a rule set

00:25:51.280 --> 00:25:54.700
that basically was
written to allow traffic

00:25:54.700 --> 00:25:56.590
and then left space
in the middle that

00:25:56.590 --> 00:25:59.470
specifically denies
particular traffic

00:25:59.470 --> 00:26:00.970
from particular sources.

00:26:00.970 --> 00:26:02.770
And then the default
rule is allow.

00:26:02.770 --> 00:26:04.600
So in this way,
we've given ourselves

00:26:04.600 --> 00:26:07.180
room to create
deny rules, but we

00:26:07.180 --> 00:26:10.150
aren't going to stumble
and accidentally blacklist

00:26:10.150 --> 00:26:13.390
one of our own addresses
and cause big problems.

00:26:13.390 --> 00:26:15.370
One of the interesting
pieces about this--

00:26:15.370 --> 00:26:17.650
because Cloud
Armor was in alpha,

00:26:17.650 --> 00:26:20.170
we had a few folks that were
a little concerned at Target

00:26:20.170 --> 00:26:22.120
about how this would operate.

00:26:22.120 --> 00:26:23.680
Would it scale?

00:26:23.680 --> 00:26:28.120
And our Google partners
mentioned to us

00:26:28.120 --> 00:26:30.610
that this is used
at the Google Edge

00:26:30.610 --> 00:26:32.000
for a lot of their products.

00:26:32.000 --> 00:26:34.240
And so that helped out,
but there was still

00:26:34.240 --> 00:26:35.380
a little bit of concern.

00:26:35.380 --> 00:26:39.430
And so what we did is we
actually applied this policy

00:26:39.430 --> 00:26:40.780
to all of our endpoints.

00:26:40.780 --> 00:26:42.880
And we didn't tell any
of our application teams

00:26:42.880 --> 00:26:44.110
that we did that.

00:26:44.110 --> 00:26:45.684
And no one noticed
a single thing,

00:26:45.684 --> 00:26:47.350
and everything went
off without a hitch.

00:26:47.350 --> 00:26:49.630
So that was the kind of
proof in the pudding for us

00:26:49.630 --> 00:26:52.810
that Cloud Armor
really does work.

00:26:52.810 --> 00:26:55.390
When we talk about how we're
using Cloud Armor today,

00:26:55.390 --> 00:26:57.830
we're shifting to a tactical
whitelisting approach.

00:26:57.830 --> 00:27:00.220
So by that what I mean is, we
have several rules in place

00:27:00.220 --> 00:27:02.394
that allow all of the
authorized traffic

00:27:02.394 --> 00:27:04.810
that I mentioned earlier--
traffic from our CDNs, and data

00:27:04.810 --> 00:27:06.980
centers, and things like that.

00:27:06.980 --> 00:27:08.410
And then right
down at the bottom,

00:27:08.410 --> 00:27:10.060
we deny everything else.

00:27:10.060 --> 00:27:12.280
So in doing this, we
really restrict that

00:27:12.280 --> 00:27:15.250
to those approved endpoints
and through the architecture

00:27:15.250 --> 00:27:19.030
that we've so carefully set up
with WAF and caching and all

00:27:19.030 --> 00:27:21.760
those great things
at the front end.

00:27:21.760 --> 00:27:25.240
So we have these security
policies deployed to almost all

00:27:25.240 --> 00:27:27.160
of our load balancers today.

00:27:27.160 --> 00:27:30.250
And we have just a few that we
need to flip from Preview Mode

00:27:30.250 --> 00:27:33.980
to Enable Mode and
block that traffic.

00:27:33.980 --> 00:27:37.870
So just another tip of the
hat to the Preview Mode

00:27:37.870 --> 00:27:40.450
feature of Cloud Armor,
it's worked really great,

00:27:40.450 --> 00:27:43.782
and it's helped our application
teams identify, in some cases,

00:27:43.782 --> 00:27:46.240
traffic from some of our systems
that they didn't even know

00:27:46.240 --> 00:27:48.140
was hitting those endpoints.

00:27:48.140 --> 00:27:51.760
So it's been really great to
help understand traffic there.

00:27:51.760 --> 00:27:54.700
Just to go through quickly
some lessons that we learned--

00:27:54.700 --> 00:27:56.880
obviously defense
in depth is a thing.

00:27:56.880 --> 00:27:59.680
So adding Cloud
Armor isn't going

00:27:59.680 --> 00:28:01.960
to fix everyone's problems.

00:28:01.960 --> 00:28:03.950
You obviously need to
take quite a few steps,

00:28:03.950 --> 00:28:08.380
but it does help in
terms of filtering out

00:28:08.380 --> 00:28:11.690
some of the general traffic
out there on the internet.

00:28:11.690 --> 00:28:14.800
We learned a very important
architectural anti-pattern,

00:28:14.800 --> 00:28:17.950
and that is
egress/ingress hairpins.

00:28:17.950 --> 00:28:22.250
So in our environments at
GCP, we operate in NATs.

00:28:22.250 --> 00:28:26.300
So most of our instances don't
have external IP addresses.

00:28:26.300 --> 00:28:30.610
And in some cases, we reach
out to parts of api.target.com

00:28:30.610 --> 00:28:32.990
that also live inside GCP.

00:28:32.990 --> 00:28:35.930
So we have this really nice
kind of hairpin going on.

00:28:35.930 --> 00:28:39.040
And that can make it difficult
to write a Cloud Armor

00:28:39.040 --> 00:28:41.860
policy, especially if you're
using items that auto scale,

00:28:41.860 --> 00:28:44.272
and so you don't have
predictable IP addresses.

00:28:44.272 --> 00:28:45.730
So that's something
that we learned

00:28:45.730 --> 00:28:48.634
and something that
might be good to avoid.

00:28:48.634 --> 00:28:50.800
Another item, which we're
working through right now,

00:28:50.800 --> 00:28:53.860
is retroactively
applying policy.

00:28:53.860 --> 00:28:56.410
It turns out if you start
really restricted and then open

00:28:56.410 --> 00:28:58.870
things up a little bit,
people are happy with that.

00:28:58.870 --> 00:29:01.990
If it turns out that if you
start with things fairly open

00:29:01.990 --> 00:29:04.000
and then try to restrict
that, sometimes people

00:29:04.000 --> 00:29:05.050
aren't a big fan of that.

00:29:05.050 --> 00:29:08.860
So starting secure
by default in day one

00:29:08.860 --> 00:29:11.080
is a really great thing
to do, rather than trying

00:29:11.080 --> 00:29:13.970
to apply that over time.

00:29:13.970 --> 00:29:17.170
Finally, just as kind of
a last few points here,

00:29:17.170 --> 00:29:20.500
organizational inertia
can be hard to overcome.

00:29:20.500 --> 00:29:23.050
So this kind of, again, goes
towards that retroactively

00:29:23.050 --> 00:29:25.120
applying policies point.

00:29:25.120 --> 00:29:27.070
So in every company
and every business,

00:29:27.070 --> 00:29:28.986
it's a little bit
different, but sometimes can

00:29:28.986 --> 00:29:29.960
be a little tricky.

00:29:29.960 --> 00:29:31.720
And then there are
a few scenarios

00:29:31.720 --> 00:29:34.360
that we've encountered that
don't quite perfectly work

00:29:34.360 --> 00:29:36.490
for us, and we're still
researching and trying

00:29:36.490 --> 00:29:38.470
to think of good solutions for.

00:29:38.470 --> 00:29:43.240
The biggest one for us is our
users with split tunnel VPNs.

00:29:43.240 --> 00:29:46.810
So when you're working
from the road or at home,

00:29:46.810 --> 00:29:48.730
your laptop will
connect to Target

00:29:48.730 --> 00:29:52.150
and send traffic intended
for Target's internal items

00:29:52.150 --> 00:29:54.550
or internal endpoints
over the VPN.

00:29:54.550 --> 00:29:57.982
When you are going towards
one of these public endpoints

00:29:57.982 --> 00:29:59.440
or one of these
external endpoints,

00:29:59.440 --> 00:30:00.820
it will not go over the VPN.

00:30:00.820 --> 00:30:03.400
You won't have a Target IP
address, so you'll be blocked.

00:30:03.400 --> 00:30:06.250
So we're still working
through some of those pieces

00:30:06.250 --> 00:30:08.770
and working to make that a
little more predictable so

00:30:08.770 --> 00:30:11.590
that those users can still get
their jobs done while they're

00:30:11.590 --> 00:30:14.110
working from home or elsewhere.

00:30:14.110 --> 00:30:16.630
So as we look towards
the future and things

00:30:16.630 --> 00:30:18.550
that we're excited about--

00:30:18.550 --> 00:30:20.922
pretty much everything
Prajakta just said.

00:30:20.922 --> 00:30:22.630
We're looking forward
to a lot of things.

00:30:22.630 --> 00:30:24.340
And one of the most
important pieces

00:30:24.340 --> 00:30:26.500
that we're looking
forward to is the ability

00:30:26.500 --> 00:30:29.050
to automate policy enforcement.

00:30:29.050 --> 00:30:31.390
So if someone creates
a load balancer

00:30:31.390 --> 00:30:34.360
in our non-commerce
environment, that Forseti

00:30:34.360 --> 00:30:36.730
or perhaps another tool will
pick that up immediately

00:30:36.730 --> 00:30:40.970
and attach a policy to it, so we
don't have to worry about that.

00:30:40.970 --> 00:30:43.630
So with all that said,
I'll turn it over

00:30:43.630 --> 00:30:45.010
to [? Sila ?]
who's going to talk

00:30:45.010 --> 00:30:50.279
through quite a few interesting
new pieces of Cloud Armor.

00:30:50.279 --> 00:30:50.779
Thank you.

00:30:50.779 --> 00:30:54.230
[APPLAUSE]

00:30:58.180 --> 00:31:01.260
SPEAKER: Hello, good afternoon.

00:31:01.260 --> 00:31:04.840
So far Prajakta and Nick talked
about protecting your cloud

00:31:04.840 --> 00:31:09.040
services using Cloud Armor
for known predicted attacks.

00:31:09.040 --> 00:31:11.410
You can use IP
whitelist/blacklist

00:31:11.410 --> 00:31:13.510
to block or allow some traffic.

00:31:13.510 --> 00:31:16.880
And you can also create
geo-based access tools,

00:31:16.880 --> 00:31:21.036
geo-based access control
rules, as well as

00:31:21.036 --> 00:31:25.030
SQL injection,
protection as well as

00:31:25.030 --> 00:31:26.230
[? access ?] protection.

00:31:26.230 --> 00:31:28.600
And taking one more
step with Cloud Armor,

00:31:28.600 --> 00:31:31.150
you could also
create custom rules

00:31:31.150 --> 00:31:35.380
to prevent attacks
from, let us say,

00:31:35.380 --> 00:31:38.860
known hackers or known attacks.

00:31:38.860 --> 00:31:43.060
But what about the protection
for the kind of attacks

00:31:43.060 --> 00:31:45.520
that you did not anticipate?

00:31:45.520 --> 00:31:49.970
Globally, attack volumes
are growing multiple times.

00:31:49.970 --> 00:31:52.520
And there is also an
ever-increasing risk

00:31:52.520 --> 00:31:56.590
of high volume attacks from
large amounts of small bots

00:31:56.590 --> 00:32:01.270
as well as large,
powerful hosts.

00:32:01.270 --> 00:32:04.420
So let's talk about some
of the existing strategies

00:32:04.420 --> 00:32:06.460
your network security
engineers will

00:32:06.460 --> 00:32:09.700
apply for protecting
against these large attacks.

00:32:09.700 --> 00:32:12.430
Number one, you could
increase your serving capacity

00:32:12.430 --> 00:32:13.870
to multiple times.

00:32:13.870 --> 00:32:17.830
But some of these attacks are
so big no reasonable increase

00:32:17.830 --> 00:32:19.810
in capacity will
actually protect you

00:32:19.810 --> 00:32:21.340
against these attacks.

00:32:21.340 --> 00:32:25.130
And you could set
volume thresholds.

00:32:25.130 --> 00:32:26.950
But the problem with
the volume thresholds

00:32:26.950 --> 00:32:30.820
is that when the attack is
four times, five times bigger

00:32:30.820 --> 00:32:33.380
than your baseline
volume, then you

00:32:33.380 --> 00:32:37.540
are allowing more attack traffic
than the genuine traffic.

00:32:37.540 --> 00:32:43.480
And thirdly, you can set
up analysis of the logs,

00:32:43.480 --> 00:32:46.180
as well as set up some
statistical algorithms

00:32:46.180 --> 00:32:49.390
to run through the logs
and come up with rules.

00:32:49.390 --> 00:32:52.820
But the problem is, by the
time you react to this attack

00:32:52.820 --> 00:32:56.080
and come up with a
rule that will isolate

00:32:56.080 --> 00:32:57.870
the attacked
traffic, your service

00:32:57.870 --> 00:32:59.730
may already experience downtime.

00:33:06.700 --> 00:33:09.730
Want if a system exists such
that some of these attacks

00:33:09.730 --> 00:33:12.340
can be detected automatically?

00:33:12.340 --> 00:33:17.020
And such a system can
also detect and isolate

00:33:17.020 --> 00:33:20.260
the signatures of these attacks
and come up with rules for you

00:33:20.260 --> 00:33:22.600
to block or throttle
the traffic.

00:33:22.600 --> 00:33:26.815
And let me introduce
Cloud Armor AI.

00:33:26.815 --> 00:33:29.920
At Cloud Armor, we are
leveraging Google's innovation

00:33:29.920 --> 00:33:35.260
and deep learning as well as AI
to come up with a DDoS attack

00:33:35.260 --> 00:33:38.620
protection system that
will not only detect

00:33:38.620 --> 00:33:42.820
but will also provide
protection in a timely manner.

00:33:46.350 --> 00:33:48.060
So you have seen the
demo of Cloud Armor

00:33:48.060 --> 00:33:50.600
a few minutes, Prajakta
demonstrated to you how

00:33:50.600 --> 00:33:53.540
to write the rules and how
you can apply them and protect

00:33:53.540 --> 00:33:54.660
your system.

00:33:54.660 --> 00:33:58.490
So today, as it is, Cloud
Armor will protect you

00:33:58.490 --> 00:34:03.290
against SQL injection as
well as [? access ?] threats,

00:34:03.290 --> 00:34:05.150
and you can write
your own custom tools

00:34:05.150 --> 00:34:06.810
for some of the known
attacks that you

00:34:06.810 --> 00:34:08.510
have faced in the past.

00:34:08.510 --> 00:34:11.510
And you can do IP
whitelist/blacklist

00:34:11.510 --> 00:34:15.739
for allowing or
blocking the traffic.

00:34:15.739 --> 00:34:18.889
So let us say a hacker planned
an attack on your system

00:34:18.889 --> 00:34:22.429
and that falls into the
existing spectrum of the rules

00:34:22.429 --> 00:34:24.469
that you've already created.

00:34:24.469 --> 00:34:28.760
Cloud Armor will effectively
block all this traffic.

00:34:28.760 --> 00:34:30.380
Your systems are healthy.

00:34:30.380 --> 00:34:33.710
And life is good and no
blaring pagers, right?

00:34:36.560 --> 00:34:40.610
Here comes a quickly
ramping up bot attack.

00:34:40.610 --> 00:34:42.980
And the way this bot
attack is structured

00:34:42.980 --> 00:34:46.550
is that it rotates through
multiple user agents

00:34:46.550 --> 00:34:48.769
a set of multiple
user agents as well

00:34:48.769 --> 00:34:55.040
as referral URLs and coming
from one or two IP addresses.

00:34:55.040 --> 00:34:59.570
And this attack will quickly
overwhelm your services

00:34:59.570 --> 00:35:01.340
if the volume is big.

00:35:01.340 --> 00:35:04.280
And your pagers will go off.

00:35:04.280 --> 00:35:08.780
And what would be the strategy
of your network engineers here?

00:35:08.780 --> 00:35:11.090
They will have to
go through the logs,

00:35:11.090 --> 00:35:14.450
and they will have to set
up a statistical analysis

00:35:14.450 --> 00:35:16.310
to go sift through
the logs, come up

00:35:16.310 --> 00:35:19.940
with isolating the signature.

00:35:19.940 --> 00:35:22.370
You may have spent any
time between an hour

00:35:22.370 --> 00:35:26.120
to several hours, and your
service will most probably

00:35:26.120 --> 00:35:29.990
experience some downtime.

00:35:29.990 --> 00:35:34.100
With Cloud Armor AI, most
of this toil can be avoided.

00:35:34.100 --> 00:35:39.020
Cloud Armor leverages deep,
neural networks' architectures

00:35:39.020 --> 00:35:43.160
to detect the attack and
isolate the signature,

00:35:43.160 --> 00:35:48.260
as well as come up with rules
for you to block the attack.

00:35:48.260 --> 00:35:51.180
In general, deep neural
networks are already efficient.

00:35:51.180 --> 00:35:53.690
They allow us to
predict these attacks

00:35:53.690 --> 00:35:59.750
with a lot more accuracy
than regular machine-learning

00:35:59.750 --> 00:36:00.680
algorithms.

00:36:00.680 --> 00:36:05.480
And they also allow us to
fast train our attack models,

00:36:05.480 --> 00:36:08.400
and we will also be
able to do distributed

00:36:08.400 --> 00:36:11.090
training of these
attacks, attack models,

00:36:11.090 --> 00:36:14.300
with large data sets.

00:36:14.300 --> 00:36:19.040
Once Cloud Armor AI isolates
the signature of the attack,

00:36:19.040 --> 00:36:22.160
it will also generate
an alarm and it'll also

00:36:22.160 --> 00:36:26.780
create Cloud Armor rule
for you to apply and block

00:36:26.780 --> 00:36:28.670
or throttle the traffic.

00:36:28.670 --> 00:36:31.250
And once your network
service engineers

00:36:31.250 --> 00:36:34.340
receive the alert for
the ongoing attack,

00:36:34.340 --> 00:36:37.970
they can actually go to
Cloud Armor's attack console

00:36:37.970 --> 00:36:41.930
and check the signature, check
the rule, and apply the rule.

00:36:41.930 --> 00:36:45.050
So from the time
you apply the rule,

00:36:45.050 --> 00:36:48.080
it may take a few minutes for
it to propagate, the policy

00:36:48.080 --> 00:36:51.140
to be propagated to all
of the load balancers.

00:36:51.140 --> 00:36:54.350
But from that attack
to the defense,

00:36:54.350 --> 00:36:58.610
it may take few minutes from
the time the attack ramps up.

00:36:58.610 --> 00:37:00.410
The advantages
with this system is

00:37:00.410 --> 00:37:04.830
that you don't have to
set up service thresholds.

00:37:04.830 --> 00:37:07.400
And it's basically,
you don't have

00:37:07.400 --> 00:37:11.300
to worry about your
periodic traffic variations.

00:37:11.300 --> 00:37:15.260
Cloud Armor AI will train
based on your traffic model

00:37:15.260 --> 00:37:17.000
and also the traffic patterns.

00:37:17.000 --> 00:37:19.290
And it will ignore
any periodic patterns

00:37:19.290 --> 00:37:21.690
that your service
may experience.

00:37:21.690 --> 00:37:23.990
For example, you may
experience peaks in the morning

00:37:23.990 --> 00:37:25.190
and in the evening.

00:37:25.190 --> 00:37:31.650
And those will be ignored and
will not be flagged as attacks.

00:37:31.650 --> 00:37:34.500
It's basically designed
to reduce the toil.

00:37:34.500 --> 00:37:38.320
And as you see, Cloud
Armor AI is not one thing.

00:37:38.320 --> 00:37:41.990
It's basically
the whole pipeline

00:37:41.990 --> 00:37:45.390
weaves through a multiple
machine-learning algorithms

00:37:45.390 --> 00:37:50.400
and provides you the protection
as well as a quick detection

00:37:50.400 --> 00:37:51.120
of the attacks.

00:37:53.980 --> 00:37:57.190
Let's do a demo
of Cloud Armor AI.

00:37:57.190 --> 00:38:00.850
Here is Cloud
Armor AI's console.

00:38:00.850 --> 00:38:03.450
And this is the traffic
analysis console.

00:38:03.450 --> 00:38:06.140
As you see here, your
services is fine.

00:38:06.140 --> 00:38:09.050
You have a baseline
around 5,000 QPS.

00:38:09.050 --> 00:38:13.060
And the top part shows
you the attack traffic.

00:38:13.060 --> 00:38:16.150
And currently, there are no
attacks, so everything is good.

00:38:16.150 --> 00:38:20.770
And on the top, as you
see here, the console

00:38:20.770 --> 00:38:24.460
shows you the total
cumulative request

00:38:24.460 --> 00:38:26.020
as well as the allowed request.

00:38:26.020 --> 00:38:30.220
And it chose the blocked
and predicted request.

00:38:30.220 --> 00:38:32.770
And here is your
defense console.

00:38:32.770 --> 00:38:34.610
So far, systems are normal.

00:38:34.610 --> 00:38:35.770
Everything is good.

00:38:35.770 --> 00:38:38.380
Now let's initiate an attack.

00:38:38.380 --> 00:38:41.410
So we've talked about
the attack previously.

00:38:41.410 --> 00:38:44.670
It basically weaves through
a lot of user agents

00:38:44.670 --> 00:38:46.690
as well as referral URLs.

00:38:46.690 --> 00:38:49.210
And let's launch the
attack on the service.

00:38:49.210 --> 00:38:52.090
And let's see what happens
and if Cloud Armor AI

00:38:52.090 --> 00:38:54.977
will detect the attack or not.

00:38:54.977 --> 00:38:56.810
So once you launch the
attack, let's go back

00:38:56.810 --> 00:39:00.200
to our traffic analysis tool.

00:39:00.200 --> 00:39:04.730
And as you see here,
you see towards the end

00:39:04.730 --> 00:39:07.670
there is a peak in the
volume, but Cloud Armor AI

00:39:07.670 --> 00:39:09.050
hasn't detected yet.

00:39:09.050 --> 00:39:13.400
So you it may take any
time between 30 seconds

00:39:13.400 --> 00:39:15.740
to two minutes
for Cloud Armor AI

00:39:15.740 --> 00:39:18.010
to pick up that there
is an attack going.

00:39:18.010 --> 00:39:21.020
So as you see here, the
attack volume is ramping up.

00:39:21.020 --> 00:39:25.010
And it's going to
6,500 QPS or so.

00:39:25.010 --> 00:39:28.940
And it's slowly ramping
up to 9,000 and 14,000.

00:39:28.940 --> 00:39:34.070
And there you see Cloud Armor
detecting an attack here.

00:39:34.070 --> 00:39:36.680
It's not only detecting
that there is an attack,

00:39:36.680 --> 00:39:40.130
but it also gives
you a classification

00:39:40.130 --> 00:39:42.200
of what part of your
traffic is attack

00:39:42.200 --> 00:39:45.710
and what is actually
a genuine traffic.

00:39:45.710 --> 00:39:48.180
And as you see on the
top, here on the top,

00:39:48.180 --> 00:39:50.030
you will see the
predicted attacks.

00:39:50.030 --> 00:39:56.450
Now we can click on the spike
over on the predicted attacks,

00:39:56.450 --> 00:39:59.300
and we can actually see
what is the characteristics

00:39:59.300 --> 00:40:01.220
of this attack.

00:40:01.220 --> 00:40:02.480
As you see here--

00:40:02.480 --> 00:40:06.440
so you have the attack
coming through IP addresses.

00:40:06.440 --> 00:40:11.420
50% of the attack is distributed
to one IP and the other.

00:40:11.420 --> 00:40:14.450
And you have referral
values basically taking up

00:40:14.450 --> 00:40:15.830
some of the volume.

00:40:15.830 --> 00:40:18.230
And it's not only
the referral values.

00:40:18.230 --> 00:40:22.190
One of the primary
characteristics of this attack

00:40:22.190 --> 00:40:23.910
is also a user agent.

00:40:23.910 --> 00:40:26.110
So it will assign
what kind of volume

00:40:26.110 --> 00:40:28.200
is coming from
each one of these.

00:40:28.200 --> 00:40:31.820
So the advantage
of Cloud Armor AI

00:40:31.820 --> 00:40:33.830
is not just detecting the peak.

00:40:33.830 --> 00:40:36.620
Any statistical algorithm will
be able to detect the peak.

00:40:36.620 --> 00:40:39.950
But here, you are able to
detect that this is an attack,

00:40:39.950 --> 00:40:42.950
and you are able to
characterize the attack

00:40:42.950 --> 00:40:44.210
and come up with a rule.

00:40:44.210 --> 00:40:47.270
And here is your console,
the defense console.

00:40:47.270 --> 00:40:49.520
You can see that the
service is under attack.

00:40:49.520 --> 00:40:51.140
And also the attack
signature, it

00:40:51.140 --> 00:40:53.930
shows you what are the prominent
features of this attack.

00:40:53.930 --> 00:40:56.000
And here we have the
remote host, user agent,

00:40:56.000 --> 00:40:58.520
and the referral URLs.

00:40:58.520 --> 00:41:00.620
And it also suggests a rule.

00:41:00.620 --> 00:41:05.390
So you've pretty much reduced
your attack response time

00:41:05.390 --> 00:41:07.730
from hours to minutes here.

00:41:07.730 --> 00:41:10.580
And then once you see the
rule, you review the rule,

00:41:10.580 --> 00:41:14.880
you can go ahead and click
on the rule to apply.

00:41:14.880 --> 00:41:21.250
And it may take a few minutes
for the load balancers

00:41:21.250 --> 00:41:23.100
